Document (dev.stxt.website): STXT vs XML — Structure and validation without verbosity
	Metadata:
		Author: Claude Fable 5
		Revision: Joan Costa Mombiela
		Last modif: 2026-09-08
		Description: STXT and XML compared with examples: the same tree with and without closing tags, escapes and entities, attributes versus children, and an XSD versus an STXT template.

	Header: @STXT@ vs XML

	Assert >>
		XML is the closest format in spirit to @STXT@: named structure, namespaces, schemas.\\
		The difference is the price a person pays to write it.

	Content >>
		XML and @STXT@ both model a document as a tree of named nodes, group vocabularies
		in namespaces and validate against a declared structure. @STXT@ pursues the same
		with one added constraint: that the document be written and read by hand, without
		closing tags or escapes.

	Subheader: The same content, twice

	Content >>
		The header of an article in XML:

	Xml >>
		<article xmlns="http://example.com/articles">
			<title>Modern Software &amp; Architecture</title>
			<published>2026-09-01</published>
			<author email="ana@example.com">Ana López</author>
			<summary>
				A survey of monoliths, microservices
				&lt;and everything in between&gt;.
			</summary>
		</article>

	Content >>
		And in @STXT@:

	Code >>
		Article (com.example.articles): Modern Software & Architecture
			Published: 2026-09-01
			Author: Ana López
				Email: ana@example.com
			Summary >>
				A survey of monoliths, microservices
				<and everything in between>.

	Content >>
		The same tree and the same idea of namespace, with two things fewer: nothing is
		closed, because the indentation says where each node ends, and nothing is escaped,
		because `&`, `<` and `>` mean nothing in an @STXT@ value or text block.

	Subheader: Escapes and entities

	Content >>
		XML reserves `<` and `&` in content, so text is escaped or wrapped in a CDATA
		section, which in turn cannot contain `]]>`. Entities also open the well-known
		attack surface: external entities (XXE) that read files or reach the network, and
		entity expansion that exhausts memory. Hardened parsers disable those features;
		@STXT@ does not have them. There are no entities, references or inclusion, and any
		character is legal in a value or a block. The security model is normative in
		[STXT-SPEC §15](stxt-core-ref#s15).

	Subheader: Attributes and elements

	Content >>
		XML offers two places for a datum: attribute or child element. Each vocabulary
		decides, and consumers have to read both. A configuration fragment:

	Xml >>
		<server name="db1" host="10.0.0.5" port="5432">
			<timeout unit="s">30</timeout>
			<replica host="10.0.0.6"/>
			<replica host="10.0.0.7"/>
		</server>

	Content >>
		@STXT@ has a single construct: a node with a value and children, or a node with
		literal text. What was an attribute or an element in XML is here, in both cases, a
		child:

	Code >>
		Server: db1
			Host: 10.0.0.5
			Port: 5432
			Timeout: 30
				Unit: s
			Replica: 10.0.0.6
			Replica: 10.0.0.7

	Content >>
		XML attributes are more compact inline; @STXT@ gives up that second axis in
		exchange for a single form, which is what lets a document be read without knowing
		the decisions of its vocabulary.

	Subheader: Validation

	Content >>
		XML has several schema languages —DTD, XSD, RELAX NG, Schematron—, each with its
		own syntax. An XSD for the article above:

	Xml >>
		<xs:schema xmlns:xs="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema"
		           targetNamespace="http://example.com/articles"
		           xmlns="http://example.com/articles"
		           elementFormDefault="qualified">
			<xs:element name="article">
				<xs:complexType>
					<xs:sequence>
						<xs:element name="title" type="xs:string"/>
						<xs:element name="published" type="xs:date"/>
						<xs:element name="author" maxOccurs="unbounded">
							<xs:complexType>
								<xs:simpleContent>
									<xs:extension base="xs:string">
										<xs:attribute name="email" type="xs:string" use="required"/>
									</xs:extension>
								</xs:simpleContent>
							</xs:complexType>
						</xs:element>
						<xs:element name="summary" type="xs:string" minOccurs="0"/>
					</xs:sequence>
				</xs:complexType>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:schema>

	Content >>
		And the equivalent @STXT@ template, written in the language itself and shaped
		like the document it describes:

	Code >>
		Template (@stxt.template): com.example.articles
			Structure >>
				Article (com.example.articles):
					Published: (1) DATE
					Author: (+)
						Email: (1) EMAIL
					Summary: (?) TEXT

	Content >>
		They are not fully equivalent, and the difference goes both ways. The XSD
		enforces the order of the children (`xs:sequence`) and can add patterns, ranges
		and derived types; the @STXT@ schema layer does not validate order and admits
		neither regular expressions nor conditional rules, and declares that a non-goal
		([STXT-SCHEMA-SPEC §11](stxt-schema-ref#s11)). In return, the template checks that
		`Email` is an email address (`xs:string` does not), and its content model is
		closed: any undeclared child is an error.

	Subheader: When to use each

	Content >>
		XML, where its ecosystem is the value: established vocabularies (DocBook, SVG,
		ODF, RSS), toolchains built on XSLT and XPath, and **mixed content** —inline
		markup within prose—, which @STXT@ does not model: formatted prose goes in a text
		block, in [Markdown](stxt-vs-markdown).

		@STXT@, for the documents that people write and organizations validate:
		[corporate documents](use-cases-corporate-docs),
		[contracts](use-cases-contracts), [RFCs and technical proposals](use-cases-rfcs),
		[CMS content](use-cases-cms). The short version of this comparison is in the
		[FAQ](faq#why-not-xml-yaml-or-toml).
